      "keep_lost": [
        "Two economists wrote this sum down this year, Hemenway Falk and Tsoukalas: \"The AI Layoff Trap\" (c01). Each firm keeps the whole saving and bears only a slice of the spending it destroys; the rest lands on its rivals (c02). One model, not yet checked by other economists. Your napkin got there first.",
        "Someone thought so. In 1930 the economist Keynes named it: technological unemployment, jobs lost because we save labour faster than we find new uses for it. A temporary phase, he called it (c07). And in about a hundred years, about now, he guessed, a fifteen-hour week (c08).",
        "In the two economists' model the usual fixes fail: a basic income, a wage cut, new firms (c05). What can work, in that model, is a tax on the machine itself (c06). Their fix. Your dial. Still unchecked. Checked by Marco.",
        "Six weeks from now you'll be the richest person this town has ever had. And its last customer.",
        "He's right, and most people would guess wrong. From 1980 to 2010, as cash machines spread across America, bank tellers didn't fall. They rose, modestly, from about 500,000 to about 550,000 (c11). The machine counted the cash. It never asked about your mother's hip.",
        "The two economists' model, this year, ends on the same fix as your napkin: not a basic income, but taxing the machine itself (c05, c06). Still unchecked, except by forty full stools.",
        "Most people would guess the cash machines killed the tellers. They didn't. From 1980 to 2010, as cash machines spread across America, teller jobs rose, modestly, from about 500,000 to about 550,000 (c11). The machine counted the cash. It never asked about your mother's hip."
      ]
